<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Franco-Dahomean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Franco-Dahomean War of 1892–1894 was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-amedee-dodds">Alfred-Amédée Dodds</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Béhanzin</a>. After Dahomey re-armed and resumed raids in the Ouémé Valley, France blockaded its coast in June 1892 and Dodds marched inland with 2,164 men armed with Lebel rifles. The Fon army, with its women soldiers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a> or Mino, attacked again and again at Dogba, Poguessa, Adégon and Cana, but lost heavily to French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charges. Béhanzin burned and abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> before the French entered it on 17 November 1892; he surrendered on 15 January 1894 and was exiled to Martinique, and Dahomey was incorporated into French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-franco-dahomean-war.jpg" alt="Second Franco-Dahomean War" width="330" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre d'Albéca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_de_Dogba-1892.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
